const title = "What to think about before commissioning a personal painting";
const description = "A thoughtful guide to choosing a reference photograph, discussing size and medium, understanding costs and planning a personalised art commission.";
const image = "/images/artwork/2026-03-IMG_6426-768x1024.webp";
---
<DiscoveryLayout title={title} description={description} image={image} kind="journal" date="2026-11-23" priorityImage={image}>
  <section class="art-wrap art-hero"><div><p class="eyebrow">Journal / Commissioning art</p><h1>{title}</h1><p class="lead">{description}</p><time datetime="2026-11-23">23 November 2026</time></div><figure class="hero-art"><img src={image} alt="Painting of a seated woman and young girl beside a blue chair, wearing hats with red ribbons" loading="eager" decoding="async"/></figure></section>
  <article class="art-copy">
    <p>A painting made with someone particular in mind can become a very personal object. It may recall a place, mark a milestone or capture a quiet moment that matters to a family. Before asking an artist to create one, it helps to think about the image, the space where the work will live and the decisions you would like to make together.</p>
    <p>The artwork pictured here is an example from Michal Rebekah's collection. It is not being presented as a record of a particular commission.</p>
    <h2>Begin with the feeling, not just the photograph</h2>
    <p>Think about why you want this painting. Perhaps you love the way two people are sitting together, the light in a family photograph or a familiar place in the background. Explaining what matters to you is often more helpful than asking for a precise copy of every detail.</p>
    <p>An artist may be able to suggest a composition that works better as a painting. Ask how much freedom they would have to simplify a background, adjust the crop or focus attention on a gesture.</p>
    <h2>Choose a clear reference photograph</h2>
    <p>If the work is based on a photograph, choose one with enough detail in the important areas. Good lighting and visible faces or gestures make a useful starting point. A photograph does not need to be professionally taken, but a very small or blurred image may limit what the artist can interpret.</p>
    <p>If someone else took the photograph, make sure you have permission to use it. Avoid sending private images of other people without their agreement. Share a few possible references rather than assuming that the first is the best.</p>
    <h2>Talk about size, medium and where it will hang</h2>
    <p>Measure the space in which you imagine the painting. An intimate picture for a bedside wall requires a different scale from a piece intended for a large living room. Think about whether the work will be framed, and leave room for the frame when taking measurements.</p>
    <p>Watercolour and oil paintings have different surfaces, textures and display needs. If you are unsure which is appropriate, our <a href="/journal/choosing-between-watercolour-and-oil-paintings/">guide to watercolour and oil</a> can help you start that conversation. The medium, available sizes and suitability of the reference need to be agreed with the artist.</p>
    <h2>Agree the practical details before work begins</h2>
    <p>Ask about the overall price, any deposit, the expected timescale, what is included in the quoted amount and when payment is due. Clarify whether framing, packaging, delivery and postage are separate.</p>
    <p>It is also worth discussing how the artist will show progress, whether adjustments can be requested and what happens if the finished work differs from your initial expectation. These arrangements vary between artists. Do not assume that unlimited revisions, an exact delivery date or a particular result are included unless agreed.</p>
    <h2>Plan ahead if the painting is a gift</h2>
    <p>A commission needs room for discussion and making. If the painting is intended for a birthday, anniversary or other occasion, share the date at the first enquiry. Ask whether the intended timing is realistic rather than leaving it until the final weeks.</p>
    <p>For a surprise, consider how you will obtain a suitable reference photograph discreetly and with the appropriate permission. You can also ask whether the artist offers a different approach if the full artwork cannot sensibly be completed before the event.</p>
    <h2>Make the first conversation easy</h2>
    <p>You do not need a finished brief. It is enough to describe the person or moment, share your possible references and say where you imagine displaying the work. The artist can explain whether the idea is suitable and what decisions remain.</p>
    <p>To explore the possibilities, read about <a href="/commissions/">art commissions</a> or <a href="/contact/">send Michal an enquiry</a> with the idea you have in mind. Every request can be discussed on its own terms.</p>
    <p><a href="/journal/">Return to the Journal</a></p>
  </article>
</DiscoveryLayout>
